// ═══ PROBLEM 1: infinite loop (Java 7 — linked list reversal during resize) ═══
// HashMap.resize() reverses linked list in each bucket.
// Two threads resizing simultaneously → circular reference!
// get() follows next pointers → infinite loop → CPU 100% → thread hangs!
// Java 8 fixed the reversal (uses tree + no reversal), but still UNSAFE!

// ═══ PROBLEM 2: lost updates (Java 8+) ═══
// Thread-1: put("SAV2001", balance1) → hashes → finds bucket → inserts
// Thread-2: put("SAV2001", balance2) → hashes → finds bucket → inserts
// Both write to same bucket without synchronization → one overwrites other!
// Or: both see bucket as empty → both insert → one lost!

// ═══ PROBLEM 3: stale reads (no visibility) ═══
// Thread-1: cache.put("rate", 7.5) → writes to main memory (eventually)
// Thread-2: cache.get("rate") → may read OLD value from CPU cache!
// No volatile/synchronized → no happens-before → stale read!

// ═══ FIX 1: ConcurrentHashMap (BEST) ═══
ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
cache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));   // per-bucket lock!
cache.get("SAV2001");                               // lock-free volatile read!
cache.computeIfAbsent("SAV2001", k -> loadFromDB(k)); // atomic!
cache.merge("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ONE, BigDecimal::add); // atomic!

// ═══ FIX 2: Collections.synchronizedMap (simple but slow) ═══
Map<String, BigDecimal> syncCache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
// Every operation locks the ENTIRE map → one thread at a time → bottleneck!
// Iteration STILL needs external sync!

